```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: VLM-serving and agent-RAG pipelines where images undergo lossless pixel-preserving re-encoding (EXIF/metadata strip for privacy, PNG re-optimization, format-normalize to lossless-WebP, client SDK PIL re-save) between source and serving tier; serving team owns byte-keyed cache while source team controls re-encoding.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: microbench-only? The L0 proves mechanism exists (100pp gap on 5 genuine lossless re-encodes: vLLM 0% hit vs SGLang 100% hit, all pixel-identical verified max_abs_diff==0) but prevalence in production traffic is UNMEASURED. Lossy CDN traffic (JPEG q90/q95, resize) — the dominant bandwidth-optimization path — shows ZERO gap (both miss correctly). Without production prevalence data, the 100pp gap may be a microbenchmark artifact; real win requires lossless re-encodes to be common, which the packet admits is the "decisive question" and unmeasured.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Org-seam: source team must inject mm_uuid (client/source-gated, explicit in vLLM code), serving team cannot fix alone; Cross-team coordination required between image source (re-encodes) and serving (byte-keyed cache); vLLM cache-key change from bytes to processed-tensor would be breaking for existing mm_uuid users; SGLang already implements processed-tensor hashing, so vLLM would need to diverge from current default mm_processor_cache behavior; No evidence source teams prioritize cache-hit optimization over privacy (EXIF strip) or bandwidth goals]
RATIONALE: Packet: "The headline magnitude rests on an UNMEASURED PRODUCTION CONSTANT: the rate of LOSSLESS, pixel-preserving re-encoding in REAL VLM-serving traffic" and "The decisive question: are LOSSLESS pixel-preserving re-encodes COMMON ENOUGH in real VLM-serving / agent-RAG pipelines to make this a material production problem, or is real CDN traffic overwhelmingly LOSSY (re-JPEG / resize), shrinking the seam toward zero?" L0 shows 100pp gap on genuine lossless re-encodes (vLLM 0% / SGLang 100%) but lossy arm shows ZERO gap. Strict-gate caveat: pre-registered gate vLLM hit <=10% missed by all-lossless 16.7% (driven by byte-identical base64 rewrap, a correct hit) but genuine-re-encode subset 0% clears. Without prevalence measurement, operational significance is insufficient evidence.
```
